      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What was Phil Taylor's original nickname before "The Power"?</h3>
            <p>Before adopting "The Power" in 1995, Phil Taylor played under the moniker <strong>"The Crafty Potter"</strong>. This was a tribute to his mentor Eric Bristow ("The Crafty Cockney") and Stoke-on-Trent's historic pottery industry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Can a darts player change their nickname during their career?</h3>
            <p>Yes. While established professionals rarely abandon a recognizable brand, players occasionally update or evolve aliases. For example, Michael van Gerwen initially competed as "The Bulldog" and "Mighty Mouse" before settling on "Mighty Mike", while Gary Anderson transitioned from "Dreamboy" to "The Flying Scotsman".</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why do darts players have nicknames?</h3>
            <p>Nicknames gained widespread prominence during the 1970s and 1980s television boom as pioneering players brought colorful pub and regional personas to televised stages. When the PDC and Sky Sports transformed modern darts presentation in the 1990s, monikers became essential branding tools for player walk-ons, merchandise, and arena crowd chants.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Who is called "The Bronzed Adonis" in darts?</h3>
            <p>Former BDO World Champion <strong>Steve Beaton</strong> is known as "The Bronzed Adonis", a moniker awarded in the 1990s by BBC commentator Tony Green honoring his year-round sun-tanned appearance, flowing hair, and easy-going stage charm.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is Dirk van Duijvenbode's nickname?</h3>
            <p>Dirk van Duijvenbode holds two famous monikers: <strong>"The Titan"</strong> (his official PDC playing moniker, reflecting his imposing height and energetic stage walk-on) and <strong>"The Aubergenius"</strong> (coined by commentator Dan Dawson in 2020 as an affectionate nod to his administrative day job at an aubergine nursery in 's-Gravenzande, Netherlands).</p>
          </div>
      </section>

